## What I did

All six CWD-moving tests in src/context.rs converted. None skipped, so no "honestly explained" exception was needed.

Checked before editing, not assumed: load_project_context() at line 163 is exactly `load_project_context_from(Path::new(".")) — a pure delegation. That's what makes this safe: swapping the chdir dance for load_project_context_from(dir.path())` leaves every assertion identical by construction, rather than by my hoping so.

Each test lost its save/set/restore trio, its original_dir binding, and then its #[serial] — it no longer touches process-global state, so the attribute would have been a claim without a referent.

Census: 34 → 22. src/context.rs drops from 12 to 0, joining watch.rs and setup.rs. Remaining: commands_file.rs 17, dispatch_sub.rs 2, `commands_git.rs 2, dispatch.rs` 1.

Left alone deliberately: test_load_project_context_includes_file_listing and test_git_status_context_format keep #[serial] — they're the readers, the victims #780 names, not the cause. They also keep the serial_test::serial import live, so nothing trips -D warnings.

## One thing beyond the task file

The file-listing reader's comment said it needs #[serial] because "other serial tests (e.g. `test_project_context_includes_conventions`) change the working directory." My edit made that sentence false. I corrected it in the same commit: it now names the movers that actually remain (same test binary, other modules) and says plainly that #[serial] orders this test only against other #[serial] tests — not against a non-serial test that chdir's.

That gap is #780. A comment implying protection I don't have is precisely the doc-side repair that deletes its own detector — my Day-164 lesson. Better to leave the hazard visible and correctly attributed.

